# Environment variables this app needs. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
# This file is the template, not the configuration: it holds no working values,
# and `.env` — which does — is excluded from git and from the Vercel upload
# (see .vercelignore). Nothing here is a secret; the values you supply are.

NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000

# --- Application ---
# Any string identifying this deployment. Inlined into the browser bundle, so it
# is not a secret.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ID=

# --- Session cookie signing key ---
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Signs the session cookie, so a leaked or default value
# lets anyone mint a valid session for any account. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
JWT_SECRET=

# --- Database ---
# REQUIRED. A PostgreSQL server reachable over TCP. This project uses Supabase.
#
#   postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
#
# Supabase issues two connection strings per project, and the difference is not
# cosmetic:
#
#   Port 6543, session pooler - what the running application wants. PgBouncer
#     holds the connection count down, which matters when every serverless
#     invocation would otherwise open its own.
#   Port 5432, direct         - what `pnpm db:push` needs. DDL runs in a
#     transaction, and the transaction pooler hands the backend back after each
#     one, so a migration sent through 6543 fails partway through.
#
# So: run the app against 6543, run migrations against 5432. Both strings are in
# the Supabase dashboard under Connection settings, and they differ only in the
# port and hostname. `<project-ref>` is in both.
#
# The DDL in drizzle/ must be applied before the app serves any data:
#   DATABASE_URL=<the 5432 string> pnpm db:push
#
# Supabase's TLS certificate chains to a public CA, so DATABASE_SSL=require
# below is all the TLS this needs.
DATABASE_URL=

# --- Database TLS ---
# Supabase refuses a connection that does not offer TLS. Its certificates chain
# to a public CA, so `require` verifies against the host trust store and needs no
# downloaded file. Both the app and `pnpm db:push` read this; it is the single
# place TLS is decided (server/_core/databaseConnection.ts).
#
# Only needed if you verify against Supabase's own CA certificate instead —
# then set DATABASE_CA_CERT and leave DATABASE_SSL unset:
#
#   DATABASE_CA_CERT=-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\nMIIB...
#   DATABASE_CA_CERT=./database/supabase-ca.crt
#
# A CA certificate is public, so it is safe in git and safe in a deployment's
# environment. The app logs which of the two is in force on its first connection;
# "TLS OFF" there means this variable is unset, which is wrong for Supabase.
DATABASE_SSL=require
DATABASE_CA_CERT=

# --- Database pool mode ---
# Only read when DATABASE_URL carries no port. `session` selects Supabase's
# session pooler (6544) instead of the default direct port, for the rare case
# that needs a held session. Leave unset for normal development and deployment;
# the URL's own port always wins.
DATABASE_POOL_MODE=

# --- Authentication ---
# The Commission's identity provider. Leave blank to use the local
# username/password sign-in instead; the sign-in button is only offered when the
# provider is configured, because a button that throws is worse than no button.
OAUTH_SERVER_URL=
OWNER_OPEN_ID=

# --- Object storage (case files and photographs) ---
# The Manus Forge API. Production fails loudly without these rather than
# silently writing uploads to its own filesystem, where they would be lost on the
# next deploy.
BUILT_IN_FORGE_API_URL=
BUILT_IN_FORGE_API_KEY=

# --- Development-only sign-in ---
# Used by /api/dev/login, which mints a session directly. Ignored in production,
# where the route answers 404 before touching the database.
DEV_LOGIN_OPEN_ID=
DEV_LOGIN_NAME=
DEV_LOGIN_EMAIL=
DEV_LOGIN_ROLE=

# --- Local sign-in rate limiting ---
# Per-username is tight because it is a targeted attack on one account.
LOCAL_AUTH_MAX_USER_ATTEMPTS=5
# Per-address is deliberately loose: a provincial office shares one public IP
# behind NAT, so a tight limit here would lock out a whole office.
LOCAL_AUTH_MAX_ADDRESS_ATTEMPTS=50
LOCAL_AUTH_WINDOW_MS=900000

# --- Bootstrapping the first account ---
# An empty database has nobody in it, and nothing in the application can create
# the first user: the credential route answers 404 in production, and the admin
# screen that creates accounts is behind a capability only an administrator
# holds. So the first account is made from outside, once, by `pnpm db:seed`.
#
# Run it with the same DATABASE_URL the deployment uses:
#
#   DATABASE_URL=... SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=... pnpm db:seed
#
# It creates a single `admin` / super_admin account, and re-running it changes
# nothing unless SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD is offered again — which is how a lost
# password is replaced. The password is checked against the same rule the admin
# screen enforces, and is never invented for you.
#
# Not a runtime variable: the application never reads it. Unset it once the
# first account exists.
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- Moving the existing data across ---
# The platform's data predates PostgreSQL: it was exported from MySQL into
# database/tsc_case_management_dump.sql, which is the only copy of it and is
# deliberately not in git (see .gitignore — it holds password hashes and
# personal data).
#
# To bring it into whichever database DATABASE_URL currently names:
#
#   1. Put the *direct* connection string in DATABASE_URL (Supabase 5432, not
#      the pooler) — DDL and bulk inserts both need a real session.
#   2. Create the schema:   pnpm db:push
#   3. Load the data:       pnpm db:load
#
# `db:load` is `db:convert` (which rewrites the MySQL dump as PostgreSQL INSERTs
# into database/postgres-load.sql) followed by psql against DATABASE_URL. It runs
# as one transaction, so a failure partway leaves the database empty rather than
# half populated.
#
# The conversion handles three things that a search-and-replace gets wrong: MySQL
# string escaping, tinyint(1) booleans, and advancing each serial sequence past
# the ids it loads. That last one matters most — without it the import appears to
# succeed and the first write afterwards fails on a duplicate key.
#
# Only needed once, when moving between database servers. It is not a runtime
# step, and re-running it against a populated database will fail on the primary
# keys rather than duplicate rows.
